# Line-ending policy.
#
# Everything in this repo is stored as LF (it already was — every tracked file
# has an LF index entry). The problem this file solves is the *working tree*:
# with `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, checkout rewrites files to CRLF, and
# then Prettier — whose `endOfLine` default is "lf" — reports every line of the
# file as wrong. One QA session lost time to 1300 phantom errors in a single
# spec for exactly this reason.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf`, so contributors keep LF on disk whatever
# their global git config says.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Files that genuinely need CRLF ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Windows refuses to run a batch file reliably with LF line endings.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf

# ── Files that must keep LF regardless ───────────────────────────────────────
*.sh     text eol=lf

# ── Binary: never touch, never diff as text ──────────────────────────────────
# `text=auto` guesses from content and is usually right, but being explicit
# means a stray byte pattern can never corrupt an asset on checkout.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.eot    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.mp4    binary
*.webm   binary

# ── Diff hints ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lockfiles are generated; collapsing them keeps review noise down.
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
